# Copy to .env.telegram-relay (gitignored - see ../../.gitignore) before `kubectl apply -k
# k8s/overlays/demo`. Same rule as ../../.env.example's own header: this documents the *shape*, never
# a real value (ago-root docs/architecture/repositories.md - "no secrets, ever").
#
# `14-07`/ago-root `adr/0070`: this deployment's outbound calls to Telegram's Bot API fail outright
# roughly half the time from this VPS (measured, DPI-style interference - see the ADR). The fix is a
# SOCKS5 relay backed by a VLESS connection, running as a sidecar in ago-chat-worker's own pod
# (k8s/overlays/demo/kustomization.yaml's own comment on that patch explains the sidecar choice).
#
# **THIS IS THE AUTHOR'S OWN PERSONAL RELAY ENDPOINT, NOT AGO-OWNED INFRASTRUCTURE.** adr/0070's own
# Consequences section says so plainly: no SLA, the author's relay-service ToS for server-side/
# commercial use has not been separately reviewed, and this is a known, accepted gap for a portfolio/
# demo deployment - worth revisiting before any real paying tenant depends on Telegram specifically.
#
# WHY A SEPARATE SECRET FROM infra-credentials (unlike every other value in this overlay's .env).
# Postgres, MinIO, Keycloak and all three Ago.Chat.* hosts pull `infra-credentials` in via a blanket
# `envFrom: secretRef` (checked: only rabbitmq.yaml and grafana.yaml cherry-pick individual keys via
# `secretKeyRef` instead). A key added to `infra-credentials` becomes a plaintext environment variable
# in Postgres and MinIO too - containers that have no reason to ever see a Telegram relay's connection
# UUID. A second, narrowly-scoped Secret means this credential exists only where it is mounted (as a
# file, on ago-chat-worker's own sidecar container - see the kustomization.yaml patch), never as an
# env var anywhere, and its own hash-suffix rotation restarts exactly one Deployment instead of the
# whole cluster (contrast with infra-credentials, where that cascade is accepted as normal - see this
# item's own report for why this one case gets the narrower treatment instead).
#
# WHY THE WHOLE CONFIG FILE, NOT JUST THE UUID, LIVES HERE. The official image
# (ghcr.io/xtls/xray-core) ships with no shell - there is no `envsubst`/sed step available *inside*
# that container to template a UUID into a ConfigMap-mounted JSON skeleton at startup. The alternative
# (a separate init container with a shell, writing a templated file to a shared emptyDir before the
# no-shell image starts) is a second image to pin and trust for a config file that has exactly one
# secret field in it. Simpler and no less safe: the whole rendered config.json, UUID included, is one
# key in this Secret, mounted directly as the file Xray already expects - nothing here is committed,
# and everything below the line is deployment configuration exactly like every other value in
# ../../.env.example.
#
# Xray's config schema (https://xtls.github.io/en/config/): one SOCKS5 inbound bound to 127.0.0.1
# (loopback only - the sidecar shares ago-chat-worker's network namespace, so 127.0.0.1 is reachable
# from the `worker` container but from nowhere outside the pod), one VLESS outbound pointed at your
# relay. Replace the three REPLACE-ME fields below with your relay's real host, VLESS UUID and TLS SNI
# - everything else can stay as written. The value must be valid single-line JSON (no literal
# newlines) because kustomize's `envs:` secretGenerator source is a KEY=VALUE-per-line format, same
# constraint every other value in ../../.env.example is already under.
#
# Pretty-printed, this is the shape being generated:
#
#   {
#     "log": { "loglevel": "warning" },
#     "inbounds": [
#       {
#         "listen": "127.0.0.1",
#         "port": 1080,
#         "protocol": "socks",
#         "settings": { "auth": "noauth", "udp": false },
#         "tag": "telegram-relay-in"
#       }
#     ],
#     "outbounds": [
#       {
#         "protocol": "vless",
#         "tag": "telegram-relay-out",
#         "settings": {
#           "vnext": [
#             {
#               "address": "REPLACE-WITH-YOUR-RELAY-HOST",
#               "port": 443,
#               "users": [
#                 { "id": "REPLACE-WITH-YOUR-VLESS-UUID", "encryption": "none" }
#               ]
#             }
#           ]
#         },
#         "streamSettings": {
#           "network": "tcp",
#           "security": "tls",
#           "tlsSettings": { "serverName": "REPLACE-WITH-YOUR-RELAY-SNI" }
#         }
#       }
#     ]
#   }
TELEGRAM_RELAY_XRAY_CONFIG={"log":{"loglevel":"warning"},"inbounds":[{"listen":"127.0.0.1","port":1080,"protocol":"socks","settings":{"auth":"noauth","udp":false},"tag":"telegram-relay-in"}],"outbounds":[{"protocol":"vless","tag":"telegram-relay-out","settings":{"vnext":[{"address":"REPLACE-WITH-YOUR-RELAY-HOST","port":443,"users":[{"id":"REPLACE-WITH-YOUR-VLESS-UUID","encryption":"none"}]}]},"streamSettings":{"network":"tcp","security":"tls","tlsSettings":{"serverName":"REPLACE-WITH-YOUR-RELAY-SNI"}}}]}
